expectations concerns just what are some  of the thoughts from parents heading  into the school year  well what i'm hearing from parents is  excitement to have another year back in  school in person but at the same time  there are concerns because we're already  seeing that there's long-term subs or no  staff assigned to classes as kids are  getting their schedule  there's questions about busing and how  kids are going to get to school and so  again it goes back to some of the same  issues we've seen year after year but  they're only worse unfortunately in some  cases especially when it comes to like  the staffing shortage which is a big  concern for families if they don't know  who's going to be teaching their child  that gets very unnerving another major  issue with ccsd has to do with safety  recently they announced an investment in  some changes at el dorado high school  clark high school cameras on buses  there's some other things down the  pipeline  is the district doing enough to protect  the the children from a parent's  standpoint  the number one thing i'm hearing from  parents is show me how it's actually  gonna work because a lot of things sound  good on paper but then when it comes to  putting it into practice here in the  clark county school district we see that  it's inconsistent from school to school  maybe one school has it one school  doesn't one thing's in being enforced  here it's not being enforced there and  that's really what parents are saying  well it sounds good  but really what is it going to look like  for my kid in their classroom you know  the district is investing money in this  what what solutions  do you believe need to be done  district-wide  well the number one thing that really  keeps kids safe is making sure that  there's enough adults on campus  and that's one of the challenges that  we're not being able to see addressed  right now and it doesn't always have to  be teachers support staff positions  whether it's volunteers which are still  a challenge to get into campuses in many  cases in ccsd  the more caring adults that we have  available on campuses the better kids  are supervised they have adults to go to  when they're experiencing problems and  can get help  and so yes these hardening of campuses  whether it's fences or cameras  security measures  those that can be good  as well as additional policing can be a  positive thing but at the end of the day  what we really need is to make sure our  campuses are adequately staffed  and have  volunteers and family engagement so that  parents are part of the solution and  that's where i think ccsd is lagging  behind right now local control that was  one of the  legislative mandate from a couple of  years ago  the state board of education is sort of  looking into this suggesting that the  ccsd is not following what it legally  has to do  from your point of view is ccsd doing  enough on that local control aspect  whether it's engagement with parents  with students  and with funding  you know it's an interesting question  because so many people say they want  decisions made at the local level but  the number one complaint that i hear  from parents is the lack of consistency  and inequitable distribution of  decisions or resources in the clark  county school district there's got to be  a way to balance that where you can make  local decisions at the school level but  also ensure consistency in a district  where people move all the time
 

